@page
@model SuperCoolWebServer.Views.PrivacyView

@{
    Layout = null;
}

<!DOCTYPE html>


<html lang="en">

<head>
    <title>Privacy Policies</title>
    <meta name="darkreader-lock">

    <!--suppress JSUnresolvedLibraryURL -->
    <link href="https://unpkg.com/@@spiritov/ds.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
    <link href="/frontend/styles/main.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
    
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/jquery@4.0.0/dist/jquery.min.js"></script>
</head>

<body class="ds-grid">
<div class="pictochat-window">
    <div class="pictochat-status">Privacy Policies</div>
    
    <div class="pictochat-message">
        <header class="ds-darkpurple-50">extraes</header>
        These privacy policies only apply to official deployments of bots that I, extraes, personally maintain.
        <br/>
        Given the fact that my bots are open source, anyone could reasonably host their own instance(s) of these bots,
        and modify them to their hearts content -- I have no control over that.
        
        <h2>
            See the privacy policies for <b><a href="#boneboard-privacy">BoneBoard (AKA Stargrid)</a></b> and
            <b><a href="#voidway-privacy">Voidway Bot</a></b>.
        </h2>
        
        
        Given the often burst-y nature of my development of these bots, as they're worked on solely in my free time,
        <b>these privacy policies may fall out of date</b>. You can see how out of date they are by comparing their
        effective dates to their recent commits on their GitHub repositories, however I will do my level best to keep
        them up-to-date.
        <br/>
        
        
    </div>
    
    <div class="pictochat-message">
        <header class="ds-blue-50" id="boneboard-privacy">BoneBoard</header>
        
        <para>
            This policy, <b>effective September 15, 2026</b>, applies to the <b>official deployment of BoneBoard</b>,
            named "Stargrid" with the Discord bot user ID of <c>1153609385646489610</c>,
            typically operating in the fan-run BONELAB Discord server, with the server ID of <c>563139253542846474</c>.
            <br/>
            If you interact with any other instance of BoneBoard, the operator of that instance is responsible for
            its data practices, and this policy does not necessarily apply.
        </para>
        
        <hr/>
        <para>
            BoneBoard is an open-source community Discord bot primarily developed for the fan-run BONELAB Discord server.
            Its source code is <a href="https://github.com/extraes/BoneBoard">available on its GitHub</a>.
            The official deployment never runs any uncommitted/unpushed code, as it pulls directly from GitHub when
            the update & relaunch command is invoked.
        </para>
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Information BoneBoard Processes</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Discord user IDs, usernames, display names, avatars, roles,
                and other basic account or server information;
            </li>

            <li>Discord server, channel, thread, message, interaction, and reaction IDs;</li>

            <li>Message content and message metadata;</li>

            <li>Message attachments and other media;</li>

            <li>Reactions and command interactions;</li>

            <li>
                Information generated through BoneBoard features, such as game scores, leaderboard information,
                submissions, votes, cooldowns, and feature state.
            </li>
        </ul>
        
        <para>
            This information can be limited on-the-fly by server operators by removing permissions from the bot user,
            as well as by using Discord's built-in command permission system to prevent users from invoking the commands.
        </para>
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>How Information Is Used</h3>
        BoneBoard processes information as necessary to provide its features and operate the bot. This includes, for example:
        <ul>
            <li>responding to commands and interactions;</li>

            <li>moderating or enforcing channel-specific rules;</li>

            <li>creating quotes or images from Discord messages;</li>

            <li>maintaining games, leaderboards, polls, contests, and similar community features;</li>

            <li>temporarily buffering messages or attachments where that feature is enabled;</li>

            <li>operating the confession feature;</li>

            <li>determining reactions, votes, or other community interactions;</li>

            <li>diagnosing errors and maintaining the bot, and</li>

            <li>preventing abuse or misuse of the bot.</li>
        </ul>
            
        BoneBoard does not use collected information for advertising or sell personal information. <br/>
        Any features that route through an OpenAI-compatible API have the traffic routed through API keys generated
        by accounts with "training on inputs" (or any other comparable option) disabled.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Logs</h3>
        
        Logs may contain information such as:
        <ul>
            <li>Discord usernames or user IDs;</li>

            <li>message, channel, or server identifiers;</li>

            <li>message content where relevant to the logged event;</li>

            <li>command information;</li>

            <li>error and diagnostic information; and</li>

            <li>information relating to actions performed by BoneBoard.</li>
        </ul>
        
        Logs are not publicly accessible, and there is a limited number stored at any time, with older logs being
        automatically deleted on startup.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Confessions</h3>

        BoneBoard includes a feature that allows users to submit "confessions" without
        publicly displaying the sender's identity.
        <br/>
        <b>A confession should not be considered anonymous from the BoneBoard operator</b>, as operational logs contain
        information that can be used by the operator to ascertain which Discord account submitted any given confession.
        <br/>
        In the case of the official deployment, if a confession is deemed to have violated the server's rules:
        I, extraes, will review the operational logs to find who sent the offending confession, and send them a message
        appropriate to the situation.
        <br/>
        Other ordinary members are not provided this identifying information through the confession feature, although
        repeated misuses may result in a publicly visible disciplinary action being taken, or moderators being informed.
        <br/>
        BoneBoard will store the confession text and timestamp, and in the case of the official deployment,
        this confession text can be used to fine-tune a large language model at any time. Before text is sent to
        be used as fine-tuning data, whole entries will be filtered out that are deemed to be undesirable or exceedingly
        personal, lustful, sexual, angry. This fine-tuning will take place on a reputable platform under an account that
        has "training on inputs" (or any other equivalent) disabled.
        <br/>
        If someone chooses the AI rewriting option for a confession, the text of their confession is also sent to the
        configured AI service so that a rewritten version can be generated.
        They are shown the rewritten version before choosing whether to submit it or re-attempt rewriting.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Artificial Intelligence Services</h3>
        Some BoneBoard features use an OpenAI-compatible artificial intelligence service.
        Depending on the bot's configuration, this may be OpenAI or another compatible service selected by the operator.
        <br/>
        When one of these features is used, relevant content may be transmitted to that service for processing.
        Depending on the feature, this may include:

        <ul>
            <li>the text of a confession submitted for rewriting;</li>

            <li>message text being evaluated by an AI-assisted moderation feature;</li>

            <li>text being evaluated for formatting or content requirements; and</li>

            <li>contextual information necessary to perform the requested analysis.</li>
        </ul>
        
        Information sent to a third-party AI provider is subject to that provider's own privacy and data-handling
        practices. The official deployment will always be used with accounts that have "training on inputs" disabled.
        <br/>
        BoneBoard does not intentionally send unrelated Discord conversations to an AI provider merely because the bot
        is present in a server.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Message Buffering & Attachments</h3>

        Some channels may use BoneBoard's message-buffering functionality.
        When enabled, BoneBoard may temporarily retain message content and download copies of message attachments
        so that the messages can later be reposted. These temporary attachment files are deleted by BoneBoard after
        they are reposted during normal operation.
        <br/>
        Messages processed through this feature may also appear in operational logs.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Discord</h3>
        BoneBoard operates through Discord and necessarily exchanges information with Discord in order to function.
        <br/>
        Your use of Discord itself is governed separately by Discord's privacy policy and terms. BoneBoard does not
        control Discord's collection, storage, or processing of information.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Information Sharing</h3>
        Neither the BoneBoard program nor myself sell personal information.
        Information may be shared only where reasonably necessary to operate the bot, including:
        <ul>
            <li>with Discord as part of normal Discord bot operation;</li>

            <li>with a configured AI service when an AI-backed feature requires it;</li>

            <li>
                with the BoneBoard operator or administrators where necessary for maintenance, moderation,
                debugging, or abuse prevention; or
            </li>
            
            <li>when required by applicable law (if that should ever be the case).</li>
        </ul>
        
        Information deliberately posted by BoneBoard into a public or member-accessible Discord channel,
        such as a quote, leaderboard entry, game result, or contest submission, will naturally be visible to users
        who can access that channel.
        <br/>
        Note that an AI-backed feature does not necessarily require the user to invoke a command or interact with
        something explicitly highlighting its AI usage, but exact code paths can be ascertained by reading source code.
        Examples of this include, but may not be limited to: Haikus and Wiki Topic assessments.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Data Removal/Privacy Requests</h3>
        If you would like to request removal of information stored by the official BoneBoard instance,
        contact the BoneBoard operator (me, extraes).
        <br/>
        Some information may no longer be independently stored by BoneBoard but may remain as part of messages or
        other content stored by Discord. Removal of information from BoneBoard does not necessarily remove corresponding
        information from Discord.
        <br/>
        Certain information may also need to be retained temporarily where reasonably necessary for security,
        moderation, abuse prevention, or legal obligations.
        
        <hr/>
        <h3>Contact</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Discord:</b>
                @@extraes (User ID 261631460727980033).
                <span style="font-size: smaller">
                    You can find me in the <a href="https://discord.gg/mjmpUR8">BONELAB community server</a>.
                </span>
            </li>
            
            <li><b>Email:</b> <a href="mailto:talkto@extraes.xyz">talkto@extraes.xyz</a></li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    
    <div class="pictochat-message">
        <header class="ds-darkpurple-50" id="voidway-privacy">Voidway Bot</header>
        <para>
            This policy, <b>effective November 12, 2026</b>, applies to the <b>official deployment of Voidway Bot</b>
            maintained by me, extraes, for BONELAB-related communities.
            <br/>
            If you interact with another instance of Voidway Bot, the operator of that instance is responsible for
            its data practices, and this policy does not necessarily apply.
        </para>

        <hr/>
        <para>
            Voidway Bot is an open-source Discord bot providing moderation tools and information about BONELAB mods.
            Its source code is <a href="https://github.com/extraes/Voidway-Bot">available on its GitHub</a>.
            The features used in a particular server depend on the bot's configuration and permissions.
        </para>

        <hr/>
        <h3>Information Voidway Bot Processes</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Discord user IDs, usernames, display names, avatars, nicknames, roles, and basic account or server information;</li>
            <li>Discord server, channel, thread, message, interaction, and audit log identifiers;</li>
            <li>Message content, timestamps, attachments, and other message metadata;</li>
            <li>Commands, button interactions, and information entered into forms, including moderation reasons and notes;</li>
            <li>Moderation actions, the accounts involved, and daily counts of messages observed from individual users;</li>
            <li>
                Information obtained from mod.io, including mod and file identifiers, names, descriptions, tags,
                uploader profiles, comments, upload metadata, and downloadable mod files; and
            </li>
            <li>Scan results, file hashes, mod barcodes, uploader associations, and other feature state.</li>
        </ul>
        Server operators can limit the Discord information available to the bot by changing its permissions and
        configuration. Discord's built-in command permissions can also restrict who can invoke commands.
        Restricting commands does not by itself stop background features such as message logging or activity tracking.

        <hr/>
        <h3>How Information Is Used</h3>
        Voidway Bot processes information to provide its features and operate the bot. This includes, for example:
        <ul>
            <li>responding to commands and providing thread-owner controls;</li>
            <li>carrying out moderation actions and notifying affected users;</li>
            <li>filtering game invites, adjusting nicknames, and operating honeypot channels to discourage abuse;</li>
            <li>recording message edits and deletions, and forwarding Discord audit log events;</li>
            <li>maintaining moderator notes and summaries of recent activity and moderation history;</li>
            <li>announcing new or updated mod.io uploads;</li>
            <li>checking mod files for malformed uploads, potentially dangerous content, or possible reuploads;</li>
            <li>flagging potentially inappropriate mod.io comments for moderator review; and</li>
            <li>diagnosing errors, maintaining the bot, and preventing abuse.</li>
        </ul>
        Voidway Bot does not use collected information for advertising or sell personal information.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Message Logging &amp; Attachments</h3>
        Where message logging is configured, Voidway Bot may copy edited or deleted messages into a configured Discord
        log channel. These records may include the original and updated text where available, the author's identity,
        the original channel and timestamp, and message identifiers.
        <br/>
        The bot may also download and re-upload available attachments, or include links to them, in the log channel.
        <b>Editing or deleting the original message does not necessarily remove the copy made by Voidway Bot.</b>
        Copies are visible to anyone with access to the destination channel, as determined by that server's permissions.
        Logging depends on the information Discord makes available, so some original content or attachments may be unavailable.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Moderation Notes &amp; Activity History</h3>
        Voidway Bot can store moderator-written notes about members as messages in a configured Discord channel,
        and retain the server, member, and note-message identifiers needed to retrieve them.
        Moderation records may include reasons, the affected member, and the moderator responsible.
        <br/>
        The bot also maintains daily per-user message counts and records of certain moderation actions, used to
        provide recent-history summaries. These counts record how many messages were observed, rather than storing
        their full text in the activity counter itself. Message text may separately be retained by logging features.
        <br/>
        Activity history is designed to cover the last 31 days. Entries dated 31 or more days ago are removed when
        cleanup runs during normal tracking or history retrieval; this is not a timed deletion guarantee.
        This cleanup does not delete moderator notes, operational logs, or messages already posted to Discord.

        <hr/>
        <h3>mod.io &amp; Mod File Scanning</h3>
        Voidway Bot retrieves BONELAB mod information, comments, and mod files through mod.io and its download services.
        It may download and inspect archives and Unity asset bundles to identify suspicious filenames, file structures,
        objects, events, or other content, and to help identify possible reuploads.
        <br/>
        The bot maintains a persistent catalog that can associate file hashes and mod barcodes with uploader profile
        identifiers, as well as processed file IDs, trusted-modder identifiers, and operator-maintained scanning rules.
        This catalog is separate from the 31-day Discord activity history and has no automatic age-based expiry.
        <br/>
        Announcements and scan reports may be posted into configured Discord channels. Reports may include uploader
        information, filenames, extracted asset information, and downloadable text reports.
        The bot's findings are automated indicators for review and may be incorrect.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Artificial Intelligence Services</h3>
        When configured with an OpenAI API key, Voidway Bot automatically sends the text of newly observed mod.io
        comments to OpenAI's moderation service to check for potentially inappropriate content.
        <b>This can happen without the comment author invoking a Discord command or interacting with the bot.</b>
        <br/>
        Flagged comments may be reported to configured Discord channels together with the author's mod.io profile,
        the relevant mod, an excerpt of the comment, and the categories and scores returned by the service.
        Comment excerpts and moderation results may also appear in operational logs.
        <br/>
        Information sent to OpenAI is subject to that service's own privacy and data-handling practices.
        The comment-checking feature sends the comment text for classification; it does not require sending unrelated
        Discord conversations or downloaded mod files to OpenAI.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Logs &amp; Storage</h3>
        Operational logs may contain usernames and account identifiers, server and channel information, command
        details, moderation reasons, message or comment content relevant to an event, scan results, and diagnostic errors.
        They are intended for the operator's maintenance and debugging use, rather than public access.
        <br/>
        The bot limits the number of local log files according to its configuration and deletes older files on startup.
        This is a file-count limit, rather than a fixed retention period.
        <br/>
        Server configuration, moderator-note references, and mod-scanning catalogs may remain stored until changed
        or removed. Messages and attachments posted into Discord, including moderation logs, notes, and scan reports,
        remain there until removed separately. Leaving a server does not automatically erase these records.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Information Sharing</h3>
        Neither the Voidway Bot program nor myself sell personal information.
        Information may be shared where reasonably necessary to operate the bot, including:
        <ul>
            <li>with Discord as part of normal bot operation;</li>
            <li>with mod.io and its download services when retrieving mod information, comments, or files;</li>
            <li>with OpenAI when the comment-moderation feature is enabled;</li>
            <li>with the bot operator or server moderators for maintenance, moderation, debugging, or abuse prevention; or</li>
            <li>when required by applicable law (if that should ever be the case).</li>
        </ul>
        Information posted into Discord is visible to whoever can access the destination channel. Depending on server
        configuration, this may include ordinary members, moderators, or people in multiple servers receiving the same
        mod.io announcement or report.
        <br/>
        Discord, mod.io, and OpenAI each have their own privacy and data-handling practices, which Voidway Bot does not control.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Data Removal/Privacy Requests</h3>
        If you would like to request removal of information stored by the official Voidway Bot instance,
        contact the operator (me, extraes). For server-specific moderator notes or log-channel messages,
        the relevant server's moderators may also need to be involved.
        <br/>
        Removing information from the bot's local storage does not necessarily remove corresponding messages or
        attachments from Discord, the original content on mod.io, or information already processed by another service.
        Certain information may need to be retained temporarily where reasonably necessary for security,
        moderation, abuse prevention, or legal obligations.

        <hr/>
        <h3>Contact</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Discord:</b>
